  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 100 AM CST Sun Nov 15 2026\n\nThe main theme for the next few days will be the persistent strong \nsoutherly breeze. For a ceiling on these winds, NBM probabilities of \nexceeding 45 mph never get much higher than a few percent, so not \nlooking at any headlines. That said, with regards to fire weather \nconcerns... the winds, dry fuels and harvesting means they will be \nelevated, though daytime humidity is only ever down to 50 to 70 \npercent. \n\nWhere winds may exceed NBM expectations are in the downslope favored \nareas near Ree Heights and the lee of the Sisseton hills. Granted, \n1/2km winds only top out around 35-40kts. There is a window where \nthe RRFS winds increase to 50kts in the critical layer at Peever, \nthough the inversion depicted in BUFKIT is far less shallow in \ncomparison to past events. This isn't until late Tuesday/Wednesday \nwith a surface low moving across central to northeast South Dakota, \nso a bit too far out for higher resolution models to help quantify \nthe intensity of the potential downslope. \n\nWarm overnight temperatures could be near records thanks to these \npersistent winds. 850mb temperatures remain a standard deviation \nabove climo, which translates into highs well into the 50s. NBM lows \nin the upper 30s to mid 40s are close to records, particularly on \nthe 18th.\n\nIn regards to the track/timing of the low, still about the same, \nwith most of the moisture falling across North Dakota.\n\nWe also continue to evaluate a system that follows for \nFriday/Saturday timeframe. While still being depicted with some \ndegree of run to run consistency in the GFS/Canadian, it was \nsignificantly downplayed in the 12Z EC. The latest 00Z EC is closer \nto the suite of other deterministic models.  Again too far out for \nmuch details, but the NBM is still depicting a transition to \nrain/snow on the backside...though right now with minimal \naccumulations.",
